## Claim
Text: "A mail-in ballot application for voting in the US presidential election which was sent to a deceased grandfather in the State of New Mexico is evidence of potential widespread voter fraud via mail-in ballots."
Claim author: Don Larkin
Claim date: September 25, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201030014600/https://perma.cc/2ARM-YUQE?type=image

## Initial Q&A
### What entity or government office is responsible for sending mail-in ballot applications in New Mexico, and what data sources do they use to generate mailing lists?
Answer: Based on the search result, the mail-in ballot applications were sent following a state Supreme Court order, and the County Clerk's office is involved in the process for returning ballots; the text states: "The state Supreme Court had issued an order on April 14th to mail all eligible voters an absentee ballot application." However, the source does not specify the precise administrative entity responsible for the mailing task nor the data sources used to generate the mailing lists.

Source URL: https://latino.ucla.edu/research/new-mexico-vote-by-mail-matters-of-the-primary-and-state-election-laws/

### Is sending a mail-in ballot application to a deceased person due to outdated voter rolls a known and common administrative occurrence in US elections, rather than an indicator of intentional fraud?
Answer: Yes, the search result confirms that sending mail-in ballot applications to deceased individuals is a recognized administrative occurrence stemming from the inherent time lag in updating voter registration lists. As political science professor Lorraine Minnite explained, "There could be a short period of time in which if you took a snapshot of a (voter registration) list there might be some names of people on there who have died yesterday or died last week. It takes election officials a little bit of time to sort those things out." Additionally, when such mailings occurred in Michigan, experts said they "aren't a cause for concern and can actually help the state update its voting rolls." Multiple studies—including a 2016 Dartmouth study and a 2012 assessment of Georgia's 2006 election—found no evidence of widespread voter fraud connected to deceased registrants, and experts consistently explained that signature matching and other verification processes serve as safeguards against any actual fraudulent use of such ballots.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/07/14/fact-check-mailing-ballots-dead-people-not-leading-voter-fraud/3214074001/

### Does a single instance of a mail-in ballot application being sent to a deceased person constitute credible evidence of "widespread voter fraud," or does it indicate an isolated administrative error?
Answer: Based on the search result, a single instance of a mail-in ballot application being sent to a deceased person is generally identified by experts as an isolated administrative error, not credible evidence of widespread voter fraud. As political science professor Lorraine Minnite explains, it can take election officials some time to update registration lists after a death, but such instances are not "threatening to the integrity of an election." Furthermore, systems like ballot signature verification are designed to prevent any resulting fraud. Studies, including a 2016 analysis from Dartmouth, found "no evidence of widespread voter fraud," and researchers have attributed similar claims to "clerical errors" or "flawed matches" between death records and voter rolls.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/07/14/fact-check-mailing-ballots-dead-people-not-leading-voter-fraud/3214074001/

### Have election security experts or official investigations found evidence that mail-in ballot fraud was widespread in the 2020 US presidential election in New Mexico or nationally?
Answer: No, election security experts and official investigations have found no evidence that mail-in ballot fraud was widespread in the 2020 election. A report from UNM and UCLA researchers explicitly "debunking the myth" concluded that such fraud is "verifiably false" and "infrequent," noting only thirty-one credible incidents of voter impersonation in over one billion votes cast nationally.

Source URL: http://news.unm.edu/news/new-reports-assess-mail-in-voter-fraud-and-propose-election-changes-in-nm

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are: sending mail-in ballot applications to deceased individuals is a recognized administrative occurrence caused by the time lag in updating voter registration lists, not an indicator of intentional fraud. Experts and multiple studies confirm that such instances are isolated errors and do not constitute evidence of widespread voter fraud. Furthermore, official investigations found no evidence that mail-in ballot fraud was widespread in the 2020 election, either in New Mexico or nationally.

The evidence is clear and sufficient to evaluate the claim, as it directly addresses the nature of the described event and its relationship to allegations of widespread fraud. No further information is lacking.

The claim asserts that a single application sent to a deceased person is "evidence of potential widespread voter fraud." The fact-check demonstrates that this event is an expected administrative matter and that experts have found no evidence to support the notion that such isolated incidents indicate or enable widespread fraud. Therefore, the knowledge from the fact-check explicitly refutes the claim's implication. The conclusion is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The mailings were sent following a New Mexico Supreme Court order to contact all eligible voters, using voter rolls that inherently contain some names of recently deceased individuals due to standard administrative delays in record updates. Election experts consistently note that such isolated mailings are a common clerical occurrence and do not constitute evidence of widespread fraud, as multiple safeguards like signature verification exist to prevent fraudulent use. Official investigations, including a [UNM and UCLA study](http://news.unm.edu/news/new-reports-assess-mail-in-voter-fraud-and-propose-election-changes-in-nm), found no evidence that mail-in ballot fraud was widespread in the 2020 election, leading to the conclusion that the claim is refuted.